00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.680
is a bit of an idea
of who are we building

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.150
these smart cities for?

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.010
What is the reason
for making them,

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:08.420
not just how you make them?

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:09.878
So I think everyone
in the audience

00:01:09.878 --> 00:01:11.450
has got a bit of
a voice in that.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.080
So thanks for joining us.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.760
So let's warm up with
a bit of a teaser.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.490
Take a look at this kind of
echocardiogram-looking thing

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.280
off to the side here,
and see if you might

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:25.664
recognize some patterns in it.

00:01:25.664 --> 00:01:28.080
Does anyone have any ideas of
what we might be looking at?

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:31.970
Yup.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:32.470
Yup.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.139
Some urban areas, some parking.

00:01:34.139 --> 00:01:35.430
They're definitely urban areas.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:36.000
You're right.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.119
In fact, if we show
the names here,

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:39.660
we're looking at
some familiar areas.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:43.150
You got the Bay Area off to the
side, New York, London, Tokyo,

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:44.295
San Francisco.

00:01:44.295 --> 00:01:47.250
So what we're looking at
here is movements of people

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.600
on their mobile devices.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.730
Every one of these
dots is somebody

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.430
that has opted in in Google
Maps on their phone for location

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:54.930
tracking.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:58.650
So you're taking that data,
anonymised it, collected it,

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.650
and you're looking at the
living, breathing city here.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.630
This is a 24-hour
cycle of where people

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.210
are going, what
they're up to, and this

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.070
is enormously valuable data.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.990
It gives us a bit of
a sense of context

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:11.590
for what we're
talking about today.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.590
There's an incredible
amount of information

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.690
that's available to us,
if we can make use of it.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:19.500
So this is for people's personal
uses on their phones, and maps,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:20.880
and seeing where they've been.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.942
But imagine how
useful data like this

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:25.150
could be to people who are
planning traffic patterns,

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:27.480
people who are trying to
plan pickup routes for waste

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.010
management, somebody
who's trying

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.930
to get a new freeway
going, somebody who's

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.690
trying to choose where the next
neighborhood the businesses

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.010
should grow in.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.240
We're seeing a growth
in mobile devices.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.240
We're seeing a growth
in IoT devices.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.920
And we're seeing a
simultaneous growth

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.774
in the ability to process
this data on a massive scale.

00:02:43.774 --> 00:02:45.690
So this is just the
beginning of smart cities.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:50.000
We're getting into a
really exciting age for it.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.272
So a question-- you
might have expected

00:02:52.272 --> 00:02:54.730
coming to a tech conference,
coming to a Google conference,

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:56.771
that we're going to talk
purely about technology.

00:02:56.771 --> 00:03:01.010
And is this what "smart"
cities mean, the technologies?

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:02.630
So, yes and no.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:04.160
Yes, in that these
are definitely

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.510
ingredients of a smart city.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.000
I mean, having access to Wi-Fi
is what gives us the ability

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.140
to collect data.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:11.810
Having RFD transit passes
are fantastic resources

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.120
for municipal planners to
know when and where are

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.580
people going, so we can
make smarter routes.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.170
Having self-driving
cars are going

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.300
to be an enormous
overhaul of what

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.490
it means to live and
be transported inside

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:23.537
of our cities.

00:03:23.537 --> 00:03:24.620
But these are ingredients.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.690
I mean, in the same way
that an ingredients alone

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.760
don't make a cake, this
isn't the smart city itself.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.500
What John I want to talk about
today is a bit of a shift.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.990
And we're wondering if
you'd join us in this shift

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:37.676
from thinking about
a couple of nouns,

00:03:37.676 --> 00:03:39.550
a couple of the technologies
of smart cities,

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.600
to a few adjectives that
might describe them.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.650
So, over the next little bit,
we're going to do a few things.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.550
One is I'm going to share a few
stories of what we've learned

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.200
at Google from some
projects that we've

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.680
been involved in that overlap
with the smart city space.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.710
So a bit of stories that
we've had some learning from,

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.135
and a bit of what-ifs.

00:04:00.135 --> 00:04:01.760
And then we're going
to hear from John.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.134
And John's going to tell us,
well, if that's the what-if,

00:04:04.134 --> 00:04:05.660
this is what it
actually looks like.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.400
John and his
company are building

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.860
the kind of infrastructures
for cities around the world

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.890
right now that let them
make good use of their data.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:15.167
And we're going to
wrap up with a Q&amp;A.

00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:17.000
So before we dive into
some of these stories

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.800
that we've learned
from, I should call out

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.992
that this session is one
in which we have Dory.

00:04:21.992 --> 00:04:23.450
So you might have
been in a session

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:25.110
this morning that has as well.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:27.170
It's a Q&amp;A tool that
we've got at Google,

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:28.970
and we're rolling it out and
trying it at this conference.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:30.380
So if you go into the
mobile app that you

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.600
have for this conference,
and you go to our session,

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.240
you might notice that
there's a Q&amp;A portion in it,

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.747
where throughout our talk,
if you see something that

00:04:37.747 --> 00:04:39.830
looks interesting, or a
question crosses your mind

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:42.350
in the moment, you can
write a Q&amp;A question,

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.630
and the rest of your
fellow audience members

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.070
can then vote it up.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:47.570
And at the end of
the session, we'll

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:50.810
mix a bit of microphone
questions and Dory questions

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.692
from our moderator who
will read them out.

00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:54.150
So if a question
crosses your mind,

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.730
feel free to hop on to your
app and ask it as we go.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:57.230
OK.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.313
So let's take a look at
some stories of what we've

00:04:59.313 --> 00:05:01.380
learned at Google from
some smart city adjacent

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.820
and overlapping projects.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:05.829
What's the first adjective
that comes to mind?

00:05:05.829 --> 00:05:07.870
The first adjective of
smart cities that at least

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:10.720
John and I when were sitting
down to chat about this

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.010
thought it was efficient.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:13.551
And I think there's
a reason for that

00:05:13.551 --> 00:05:16.277
is because it comes up the most
regularly for smart cities.

00:05:16.277 --> 00:05:17.110
Take a look at this.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:21.280
This is a list of the most
common smart city projects

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.034
that are under way
right now in the world.

00:05:24.034 --> 00:05:25.450
And if you're
taking a look at it,

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.783
you'll probably notice a bit
of a pattern to it as well.

00:05:27.783 --> 00:05:30.190
You've got parking, street
lighting, waste and energy

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.620
management, and then a whole
bunch of transportation

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.450
logistics and optimization.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:36.100
These are efficiency problems.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.410
And one part of
it might be yeah,

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:40.160
these are the low-hanging fruit.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.300
These are areas where an IoT
sensor or actuator can really

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.420
help and not be too
complex of a rollout.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.900
But another big reason for this
is these are areas of high ROI.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:52.210
Each one of these
is making the city

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.250
better able to spend
its money or better

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:55.990
able to allocate its resources.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:59.170
And a really important
adjective of a smart city

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:02.479
is one that is not wasteful
with its resources.

00:06:02.479 --> 00:06:05.020
So what does an efficient smart
city look like in the project

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:06.940
that we've worked on?

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:10.166
Some of you might remember
this demo from Next 2017.

00:06:10.166 --> 00:06:11.290
This is a bit of a what-if.

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:12.220
This is something
that we showed,

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:13.980
which is taxi cabs
driving around the city.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:15.771
So we've got some
simulated fleets of taxis

00:06:15.771 --> 00:06:16.615
across New York.

00:06:16.615 --> 00:06:18.490
And these taxis are
kitted out with something

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:19.600
that's a little different.

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:21.730
And instead of having
just a display ad

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:25.544
in the back that's showing you
a clip or an ad for something,

00:06:25.544 --> 00:06:26.710
they're also location aware.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.930
They know where they're picking
up, where they're headed,

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:29.888
what time of day it is.

00:06:29.888 --> 00:06:32.390
And with just a little
bit of that information,

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:34.630
we were able to personalize
the ads to whoever

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:36.796
might be riding in the car
with an incredible amount

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:37.480
of accuracy.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:39.520
It's a tiny bit of
additional data,

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:41.810
but it produces a huge outcome.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:45.160
So this is an example for
ads, but imagine this kind

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.370
of intelligence if it were
applied to real-time traffic

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:49.110
control.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:52.420
If your city, or you're
building a tool for a city,

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.990
was able to equip the city's
traffic-control measures

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:59.320
to respond to floats in
parades, to respond to an event

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.810
that spontaneously happens,
to respond to a live traffic

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:02.900
conditions.

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:04.720
Or imagine if this
kind of real-time data,

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:06.820
and a little bit of
location and time awareness,

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:10.880
was able to affect
waste collection.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:12.967
So that's exactly
this next example.

00:07:12.967 --> 00:07:15.550
This is a company called Enevo,
and they're a Google customer.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:17.140
And they're building
something pretty cool, which

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:18.850
is a waste management solution.

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:21.400
And instead of being
the traditional take

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:23.830
on waste management, which
is usually sort of schedule

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:24.580
oriented--

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.960
you know, you know
your time when

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.300
you're going to get a
pickup, truck comes by,

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:30.258
they pick up your bin
whether it's full or not,

00:07:30.258 --> 00:07:31.474
and they drive off--

00:07:31.474 --> 00:07:32.890
Enevo decided that
they were going

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:35.650
to add some sensors and a bit of
an intelligence to the process.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:39.580
So they're parking-- or rather,
their waste management solution

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.490
is aware of how much
trash is in the bin.

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:45.520
They sell this to companies that
need a more efficient pickup

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:46.087
schedule.

00:07:46.087 --> 00:07:47.920
And all they did was
put a couple of sensors

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:50.290
in to know when trash
is getting full,

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:52.660
and slapped a GPS
on their trucks,

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:54.400
and out of a small
change like that,

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.050
their customers
got better service

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:58.210
because they had reliable
pick up and didn't

00:07:58.210 --> 00:07:59.620
have to think
about scheduling so

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:02.940
much as starting to think about
waste management as a service.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.440
And they themselves got better
optimization out of the route

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:07.814
because they could tell their
drivers exactly where to go

00:08:07.814 --> 00:08:09.720
and where they were needed.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.610
So what's another adjective that
might give us a bit of a place

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:15.670
to start for smart cities?

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:17.160
Participatory.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.440
So what would that mean?

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:19.940
There are a couple
of perspectives

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:20.898
you could take on this.

00:08:20.898 --> 00:08:23.240
And one of them is
participation of the people that

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:25.140
live in the cities themselves.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:27.230
So here's another project
that we had some overlap

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:28.656
with at Google.

00:08:28.656 --> 00:08:30.030
Some of you in
the audience might

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.850
recognize where in the world
we are flying over right now.

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:33.870
This is Japan.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:35.669
And specifically,
this is an image

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:38.130
that was captured
back in 2011 when

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:39.960
in the Fukushima
area of Japan there

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:43.020
were three nuclear
reactors that melted down.

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:46.420
And it was a major
public safety concern.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:49.020
And there was a pretty
incredible response in 2011.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:51.840
What we're looking at here is
a couple of people in Japan

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:55.020
that were citizens put
together Geiger counter kits.

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.960
They were makers and
tinkerers and shipped them out

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.127
to people, other citizens
around the country, who

00:09:01.127 --> 00:09:03.210
got them, built them,
connected them up to the web

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.850
on a platform called
Patch Bay at the time,

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.089
and overlaid it on
top of Google Maps.

00:09:08.089 --> 00:09:09.630
And what you're
seeing here is people

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.060
getting involved in
producing and engaging

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:14.640
with the data of their own
cities to make them smarter.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.200
This was real-time intelligence
on, should I be in my area?

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.285
Am I safe?

00:09:20.285 --> 00:09:22.410
Now, this is an interesting
and personal one for me

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:23.970
because this
platform, Patch Bay,

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:27.480
went on to become the Xively IoT
enterprise platform that Google

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:29.610
just bought earlier
this year and is

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:34.402
getting rolled in as part of
the IoT Core Cloud offering.

00:09:34.402 --> 00:09:35.860
The next one here
is Sidewalk labs.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:38.443
You might have heard of this one
as a sister company of Google

00:09:38.443 --> 00:09:39.580
underneath Alphabet.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:41.874
And Sidewalk has
a mandate to not

00:09:41.874 --> 00:09:44.290
think about the city of next
year or three years from now.

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:46.164
They're thinking about
what might cities look

00:09:46.164 --> 00:09:47.602
like 20 or 40 years from now.

00:09:47.602 --> 00:09:49.560
So this is another project
we're involved with.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.690
It gives a bit of an idea
of where might we be headed.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:55.054
And Sidewalk's taking on a
really ambitious project.

00:09:55.054 --> 00:09:57.220
It's another one that's a
little bit personal to me,

00:09:57.220 --> 00:09:59.590
as I was in Toronto
by myself, growing

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:03.220
up not too far from that
watercolor toothpick

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:05.120
on the left there.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:08.410
This is a micro-city
that Sidewalk's

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:09.480
taking on in Toronto.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.438
They're going to wire up
absolutely everything,

00:10:11.438 --> 00:10:13.030
from the municipal
infrastructure,

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:17.050
to the transportation, to
waste management, to lighting.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.032
And they're going
to try to figure out

00:10:19.032 --> 00:10:20.240
what can we do with the data.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:21.670
So this story is just beginning.

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:23.236
The Sidewalk story
is just starting.

00:10:23.236 --> 00:10:24.610
But what would it
look like if we

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:26.920
wanted to try to effect
some of these changes

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:29.560
and play with some of
these ideas right now?

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:31.480
We've done some of these
projects as Google.

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:34.930
What are some of the tools
as GCP that we're rolling out

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:38.650
so that we can help companies
and municipalities try

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:40.490
some of these ideas themselves?

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:43.420
So how might I tackle efficiency
in a participatory city?

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:47.760
Let's take a look at Google
Cloud IoT for a minute.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:50.340
So like much of
GCP, we're building

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:52.350
on the exact same
tools and stacks

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:54.090
that we build on for
our own products.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:57.474
When you're looking at the
Google Maps infrastructure,

00:10:57.474 --> 00:10:58.890
you're looking at
the same company

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:01.400
that's producing GCP tools
for your company to use.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:04.050
And we took the same
approach with IoT.

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:07.080
This is an example of what
an infrastructure might

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:09.390
look like for powering
a smart city project,

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:11.617
using a couple of
GCP building blocks.

00:11:11.617 --> 00:11:13.200
So on the left, we've
got our devices,

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:17.020
whatever they might be, whether
those are occupancy sensors,

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:19.170
they're smart
energy meters, they

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:21.762
are watching out for
traffic patterns.

00:11:21.762 --> 00:11:23.220
These are the
devices, on the left,

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:25.410
that have something
to say or that might

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:28.020
be controlled like a stoplight.

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:30.600
And the tool that we put
together in the middle

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:32.682
here is Cloud IoT Core.

00:11:32.682 --> 00:11:34.890
So this is a recent product
we've started rolling out

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:36.510
specifically for this use case.

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:40.260
There are a lot of GCP ingestion
tools for huge amounts of data,

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:41.640
like Pub/Sub.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.680
But the unique thing
about Cloud IoT Core

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:45.600
is that it's also
meant for talking back,

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:47.680
and it's specifically
meant for devices.

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:49.812
So this is something that
can run with any hardware

00:11:49.812 --> 00:11:51.645
that your city is
bringing along, any sensor

00:11:51.645 --> 00:11:54.540
or actuator, over a number
of different protocols.

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:57.990
MQTT is a lightweight protocol
for devices that are a bit more

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:00.450
resource constrained,
HTTP for something that

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.400
has a bit more compute
power and overhead

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:04.594
and doesn't need quite that.

00:12:04.594 --> 00:12:06.510
But this is a way for
you to give your devices

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:09.780
a unique identity, and be
able to talk back and forth

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:13.040
with them, and tie them into
the rest of the GCP ecosystem

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:14.790
so you can start making
use of their data.

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:16.206
And we're going
to talk a bit more

00:12:16.206 --> 00:12:18.090
about the rest of
this picture of what

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:22.540
is making use of their data
look like just in a minute.

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:25.270
But we're looking at here
is a bit of add these blocks

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:27.280
together and we can start
playing with the ideas

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:29.636
that we were looking at in
a couple slides earlier.

00:12:29.636 --> 00:12:32.010
All right, let's take a look
at a couple more adjectives.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:33.370
Responsive.

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:35.950
So responsive
seems self-evident.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:37.360
You would want a
smart city to be

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:39.860
one that can correct if
something's going wrong,

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:43.750
something that's aware of what's
happening in its own grounds,

00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:47.210
and something that
feels like it's not just

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:50.020
operating on autopilot.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.061
What's an example of
something responsive?

00:12:53.061 --> 00:12:54.560
Well, here's an
example of something

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.226
that we've learned a
bit from at Google.

00:12:56.226 --> 00:12:58.730
And this is a parking feature
that we've rolled out,

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:01.070
which is based entirely
on machine learning.

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:03.050
This isn't the same
level of granularity

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:05.520
as what John is about to
talk to us about in a minute.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:06.625
This is just high level.

00:13:06.625 --> 00:13:08.000
This is just a
bit of information

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:10.497
on where people are
sometimes and trying

00:13:10.497 --> 00:13:12.830
to make some estimations on
traffic patterns and parking

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:14.007
patterns based on that.

00:13:14.007 --> 00:13:15.590
So if I'm planning
a trip to the mall,

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:17.089
or I'm going to
city I've never been

00:13:17.089 --> 00:13:19.220
to, I can get a bit
of an idea of, hey,

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:22.742
it looks like I should go at
10:00 AM instead of 4:00 PM.

00:13:22.742 --> 00:13:24.200
And this is an
example of something

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:26.449
that doesn't just show you
where the parking lots are.

00:13:26.449 --> 00:13:30.050
It's responsive to when
you might want to be there.

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:31.340
What's another adjective?

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:32.570
Self-aware.

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:36.320
So this is one that I find
really interesting and really

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:38.460
important in the
smart city space.

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:42.410
And its core to rolling out any
new programs as a municipality,

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:45.071
or its core to being a vendor
to the cities, because you can't

00:13:45.071 --> 00:13:46.820
make good decisions
unless you know what's

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:49.240
going on your own grounds.

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:51.060
What does self-aware
look like for a city?

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:53.970
Well, a lot of you I bet
have used Street View before.

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:55.770
I was just doing a
trip last weekend

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:57.780
and had never been
south on Highway 1

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:00.420
before and was using Street View
to check out a couple of towns

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:02.940
and where's a nice
spot for a hotel.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:06.600
So, that's an example of how I
can get a bit of an awareness

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.070
as a consumer.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:11.010
But one thing that we learned
at Google from Street View

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:15.049
was the incredible
richness of image data.

00:14:15.049 --> 00:14:17.340
And this picture that we're
looking at was useful to me

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:19.131
as somebody taking a
ride, but how could it

00:14:19.131 --> 00:14:21.799
be useful to a city?

00:14:21.799 --> 00:14:23.590
You might also be
familiar with Google Lens

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:25.140
as a consumer,
something else that

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.360
makes a bit of
intelligence from images.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:29.640
This is an example of, I
take a picture of a flower.

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:31.810
I can have that flower
identified for me.

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:34.230
It was something that
rolled out at a recent I/O

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:37.350
and is chasing that exact
same problem of, well, we've

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:40.120
got a lot of image data
in cities these days.

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:41.750
Is there anything
we can do with that?

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:43.240
So this is a consumer example.

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:46.110
What might that look like
in a smart city context?

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.810
How can I take these adjectives,
responsive and self-aware,

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:50.520
and look at some
Google tools that

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:53.407
would let me chase that, that
would let me learn from that?

00:14:53.407 --> 00:14:55.990
So I'm pretty excited to be able
to chat about this one today,

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:58.300
as it's a relatively new
idea that some of our teams

00:14:58.300 --> 00:14:59.770
have been playing around with.

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:01.240
And it's a combination
between some

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:04.150
of our learnings from
Street View, and our machine

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:06.520
learning and image
processing tools,

00:15:06.520 --> 00:15:09.460
and the GCP tools that
are offered publicly.

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:12.580
So none of this is you have
to ask for special permission.

00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:15.410
This is all GCP
tool capabilities.

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:17.590
But it's an idea that
we're looking actively

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:18.547
for early partners on.

00:15:18.547 --> 00:15:20.880
So if it looks like something
that's interesting to you,

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:23.530
reach out to me after the talk.

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:25.550
You know Lens is the
consumer product.

00:15:25.550 --> 00:15:28.190
It will take an image
and tell you a rough idea

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:29.170
of what's in the image.

00:15:29.170 --> 00:15:32.000
What you might also know
is that inside GCP there

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:35.110
are a couple image processing
tools that go deeper than that.

00:15:35.110 --> 00:15:38.690
The Vision API is effectively
that for your apps.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:42.110
If you're building something
that has a photo capability,

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:44.570
you can send the image
to the Cloud Vision API,

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:46.910
and it will return with
a level of confidence

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:48.620
what it thinks is in that image.

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:50.394
And that's great for
a lot of use cases.

00:15:50.394 --> 00:15:52.560
If you want to be able to
tell, yeah, this is a car,

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:54.625
or this is a park,
or this is a tree,

00:15:54.625 --> 00:15:56.000
it's an incredibly
powerful thing

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:59.417
to build in with absolute
simplicity to an app.

00:15:59.417 --> 00:16:01.250
But what if I wanted
to go one level deeper?

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:02.780
What if a city
really wanted to get

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:05.270
to know what's going
on on its grounds

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:07.655
without having to kit out
an enormous sensor network?

00:16:07.655 --> 00:16:10.380
Well, let's go one level deeper
to the next product called

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:11.529
AutoML.

00:16:11.529 --> 00:16:13.820
And this is where you can
start training your own image

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:15.350
recognition models.

00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:17.540
So this starts to
get really powerful

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:20.330
as cities start wanting to
ask questions about what's

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:22.070
going on our grounds.

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:24.770
Now, our own Street View
imagery isn't publicly available

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:27.710
and isn't available to process
for a number of privacy

00:16:27.710 --> 00:16:28.490
reasons.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:31.850
But images that a
city is taking itself

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:34.370
are well within their
right to process.

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:37.072
So, what might it look
like to take a look for,

00:16:37.072 --> 00:16:38.780
are there trees close
to power lines that

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:40.220
might be a risk right now?

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:42.710
Are there potholes in
the road that we might

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:44.840
need to be keeping track of?

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:46.820
These two products are
built for exactly that.

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:48.620
Cloud Vision API
gives you a good idea

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:51.570
of what's in a picture and
is dead simple to integrate

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:52.860
your applications.

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:53.990
You pass it an image.

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:56.440
It passes back what it
thinks is in that image.

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:58.244
But if you want to
go one level deeper,

00:16:58.244 --> 00:17:00.410
and you're working on an
application for a city that

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:04.140
says, we'd love to help you
answer a very specific problem,

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:09.440
then Cloud AutoML will let you
very simply, without a data

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:12.380
scientist on your team,
create new models that

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:14.849
will allow you to identify
what you're looking for.

00:17:14.849 --> 00:17:16.790
So imagine what this
can do for cities.

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:19.190
If you could get a better
idea of real estate

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:20.640
value out of images.

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:22.369
If you could take
a look at where

00:17:22.369 --> 00:17:25.460
are we at risk for a
tree falling on a line?

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:28.460
Where are we looking promising
for new areas of growth

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:30.440
based on businesses
and buildings that

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:32.440
are booming in that area?

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:34.790
An idea of, could we
look for indicators

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:37.070
of energy surge and production?

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:40.670
Could we look for measures
quantitatively of development,

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:43.467
of poverty, of living
conditions in our cities?

00:17:43.467 --> 00:17:45.050
These are tools that
are on GCP today.

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:50.220
So, we've talked a bit about
a couple of the projects

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:51.730
that we're working on at Google.

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:54.600
And we've talked a bit about
what we learn from them,

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:56.100
and what are some
of the GCP tools

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:59.590
that might help you ask those
same questions in your cities.

00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:01.240
Let's go one level deeper.

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:03.660
Imagine if you were never
stuck looking for parking.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:05.880
And to answer that
question, please join me

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:08.265
in welcoming up John.

00:18:08.265 --> 00:18:12.065
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:13.015 --> 00:18:15.030
JOHN HEARD: Thanks, Fraser.

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:18.310
Yeah, we're living in a
changing world, aren't we?

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:20.440
And parking is one
of the things that we

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:22.720
live with which really sucks.

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:27.160
And so, I just want to tell you
a little bit about who we are

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:31.840
and what we've been
doing for now 15 years.

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:34.330
We've had to build a lot
of technology ourselves

00:18:34.330 --> 00:18:36.010
because there are no standards.

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:40.660
But we're doing it in the
real world, everyday, today.

00:18:40.660 --> 00:18:45.580
So, we're based out of several
locations around the world--

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:48.670
Australia, New
Zealand, and the UK.

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:51.190
We actually dogfood our
own technology in the UK,

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:55.330
and we run hundreds and hundreds
of carparks across the UK

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:56.700
with our own technology.

00:18:56.700 --> 00:19:00.260
But in New Zealand and Australia
and other parts of the world,

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:05.530
we have a number of very
significant smart parking

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:09.340
systems in place, tens
of thousands of sensors.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:13.660
We build sensors that go in
the ground just like that.

00:19:13.660 --> 00:19:16.090
That's an example of one.

00:19:16.090 --> 00:19:19.450
It's not much bigger
than a D-size battery.

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:23.884
And it has to last for up
to 10 years in the ground.

00:19:23.884 --> 00:19:25.550
So that's the sort
of things that we do.

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:26.860
So we've had to do hardware.

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:28.000
We've had to do software.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.700
We have to do
networking, et cetera.

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:33.630
And it's all powered today
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:36.490 --> 00:19:39.280
This is just a quick list
of some of the cities

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:44.440
that today are using the
smart parking solution.

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:48.104
And this is a still a
journey in progress.

00:19:48.104 --> 00:19:50.020
You know, a lot of the
things that we're doing

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:52.150
are still just the
very basics of,

00:19:52.150 --> 00:19:54.990
just show me where
a car park is.

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:59.860
Tell the city or the business
how the parking is used.

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:04.930
Is it being used efficiently and
effectively and continuously,

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:06.220
or is it underused?

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:07.540
Or is it overused?

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:10.590
Is the pricing right?

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:13.720
Are the types of rules,
such as how much time

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:16.780
you're allowed to park in a
certain parking spot, actually

00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:18.430
appropriate, et cetera?

00:20:18.430 --> 00:20:20.590
So, these are just
some of the cities

00:20:20.590 --> 00:20:23.000
that we're running today.

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:25.825
And the big question
we're starting to pose

00:20:25.825 --> 00:20:29.800
is what about your city?

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:35.080
So, today we already,
as you can appreciate,

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:37.270
we've had to build a
comprehensive range

00:20:37.270 --> 00:20:38.050
of solutions.

00:20:38.050 --> 00:20:39.940
And this diagram
just illustrates

00:20:39.940 --> 00:20:42.840
some of the types of
functionality targets

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:48.490
or audiences that this
technology delivers to.

00:20:48.490 --> 00:20:51.220
First of all, the person
in the car driving.

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:54.550
We deliver a broad
range of applications

00:20:54.550 --> 00:20:57.910
which give guidance and
information about parking

00:20:57.910 --> 00:20:58.440
spaces.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:01.810
It's using Google Maps, using
Street View, all that sort

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:05.350
of interactivity, that
convenience, that connection

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:08.290
with you, so that you understand
what's actually happening

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:09.400
and where are you going.

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:13.180
And then, finally, being able
to actually pay for that parking

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:16.090
session, whether it be
a pay-for-what-you-use--

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:20.020
so a timer starts to
just tick away the time,

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:22.450
and when you've finished
your car-parking session,

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:24.960
and you drive away, it
closes that session.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:26.050
We do that today.

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:29.620
Or it can be a fixed prepaid--

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:32.630
you pay for 20 minutes, or pay
for 30 minutes, or whatever.

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:35.350
So that's one class
of application

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:36.700
that we are delivering.

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:40.150
And just last week, we
delivered two new applications,

00:21:40.150 --> 00:21:41.980
two new versions
of that application

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:45.430
to customers in other cities.

00:21:45.430 --> 00:21:47.210
The enforcement officer.

00:21:47.210 --> 00:21:51.100
I know this is probably
the least desirable aspect

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:51.615
of parking.

00:21:51.615 --> 00:21:53.740
But there are people that
need to actually go along

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:57.940
the streets and effectively
and efficiently manage where

00:21:57.940 --> 00:22:01.860
the cars that are just not
playing by the rules, right,

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:04.000
and do the necessary
thing of putting

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:05.500
a ticket on your window.

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:08.680
Now, what we give is live,
real-time information

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:11.140
to those officers on
the street telling them

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:13.690
exactly where the car is,
exactly how much they paid,

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:16.260
when it expire, et cetera,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:19.670
And this is real-time
information.

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:22.930
A lot of what Fraser
was just talking about

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:24.400
is about being alive.

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:25.870
The city is alive.

00:22:25.870 --> 00:22:31.500
And these sort of things
need to be constantly moving.

00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:37.630
Talking about IoT, one of
the things that we have found

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:41.700
is a necessity of parking
is that this is actually

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:44.340
legally enforceable IoT.

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:47.200
As you well know,
as being drivers,

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:50.100
and you've ridden in
cars and parked in cars--

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:51.660
we all have, right--

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:54.660
a car can actually leave a
parking space and another one

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:56.290
pull in right away, right?

00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:59.070
And we're talking about,
wouldn't the second--

00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:02.070
assuming you're not
speeding too fast,

00:23:02.070 --> 00:23:04.230
but it happens
really, really fast.

00:23:04.230 --> 00:23:07.650
And if we didn't detect
when that new car comes in,

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:11.370
we could give a parking
ticket to that second arrival.

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:12.420
We can't allow that.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:15.690
And this is actually the
rigor and the significance

00:23:15.690 --> 00:23:18.750
of the class of IoT
that parking is.

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:20.720
Now, also as I
commented before, this

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:22.020
is a battery-operated thing.

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:23.850
And it has to last for years.

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:26.400
But meanwhile, we're doing
this real-time communications.

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.460
We've buried it in the ground,
and that's usually in concrete,

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:31.880
or tarmac cement, and so on.

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:34.570
It is very, very nasty
for radio communications.

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:36.900
We've had to develop that
type of radio communications.

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:38.220
And finally, we put
big metal things

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:39.750
over the top of
it, which also gets

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:44.430
in the way of doing reliable,
secure, robust radio

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:45.270
communications.

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:49.350
So, this is the class,
the edge of IoT,

00:23:49.350 --> 00:23:53.190
that parking is all about
and smart parking has solved.

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:57.060
And I can tell you, we've got
a lot of wounds on our backs,

00:23:57.060 --> 00:24:00.510
a lot of scars from
learning this technology.

00:24:00.510 --> 00:24:04.350
And in fact, as I commented,
dogfooding our own technology

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:06.720
has been a really,
really good way

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:08.980
to make sure that we
do the right thing.

00:24:13.610 --> 00:24:16.520
What has actually started
to come back to us

00:24:16.520 --> 00:24:18.530
from our customers,
from those cities,

00:24:18.530 --> 00:24:23.630
from those customers who are
utilizing our smart parking

00:24:23.630 --> 00:24:26.420
platform, is once they put those
gateways, those communication

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:30.190
gateways, onto their lampposts,
getting power and network

00:24:30.190 --> 00:24:32.900
connectivity to them,
either through 3G, 4G,

00:24:32.900 --> 00:24:36.860
or some sort of other
communications mechanism,

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:40.670
that gateway becomes a very
strategic element for building

00:24:40.670 --> 00:24:45.410
out more sensing and more
capability into the smart city

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:49.160
infrastructure and the
solutions that a city wants

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:50.150
to actually deliver.

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:54.200
And so, what we've been actually
being told by our customers

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:56.630
and we're responding
to, is building

00:24:56.630 --> 00:24:59.090
an open platform,
an open framework,

00:24:59.090 --> 00:25:01.460
which enables smart cities.

00:25:01.460 --> 00:25:03.260
And so it's not
just about parking.

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:06.980
It's actually about
modern life in cities,

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:11.180
pretty much as Fraser and
I are talking about now.

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:15.600
And where this is
taking us is actually

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:19.590
into building a broad
range of solutions

00:25:19.590 --> 00:25:23.010
that sit on top of the
smart cloud platform, which

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:24.780
is completely GCP.

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:29.290
It's actually 100% pure
serverless as well.

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:31.440
There is no Linux
operating system in it.

00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:34.140
There is no windows
operating system in it.

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:39.385
It is completely built utilizing
GCP serverless computing

00:25:39.385 --> 00:25:39.885
components.

00:25:39.885 --> 00:25:41.460
And this is really
exciting because I

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:44.730
believe this is actually
the future for how we build

00:25:44.730 --> 00:25:50.670
large-scale, internet-scale,
global-scale, live network

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:56.230
computing systems, which
is essentially what

00:25:56.230 --> 00:25:57.760
I'm going to talk about here.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:26:00.310
Now, the first building
block that we utilized

00:26:00.310 --> 00:26:04.930
was the Cloud Core
IoT functionality

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:07.060
that Google provided.

00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:08.020
And we did this.

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:11.810
We started using it at
around about two years ago.

00:26:11.810 --> 00:26:13.990
Prior to that, we'd
actually been building our,

00:26:13.990 --> 00:26:17.290
own IoT framework for
managing our devices.

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:19.450
But as we were listening
to our customers

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:23.110
and seeing the need
for smart cities,

00:26:23.110 --> 00:26:26.440
we needed an open IoT framework.

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:29.860
And it just did not make
sense to reinvent that wheel,

00:26:29.860 --> 00:26:33.940
especially when you had solid
serverless technology that was

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:36.250
also going to be
driven by the market,

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:40.030
that was going to be open and
flexible, that we didn't--

00:26:40.030 --> 00:26:41.950
our team didn't need to rebuild.

00:26:41.950 --> 00:26:43.420
We didn't even
need to support it.

00:26:43.420 --> 00:26:46.210
We don't need to now
maintain that technology.

00:26:46.210 --> 00:26:49.000
And we can trust
that GCP and Google

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:51.430
are going to drive
that more and more

00:26:51.430 --> 00:26:54.730
into the functionality
requirements of the market.

00:26:54.730 --> 00:26:58.669
And we just are able
to benefit from it.

00:26:58.669 --> 00:27:00.460
And as I commented
before, this is actually

00:27:00.460 --> 00:27:05.560
giving us city-scale,
live responsiveness.

00:27:05.560 --> 00:27:09.730
We are doing today with those
cities that I listed before,

00:27:09.730 --> 00:27:12.430
in the order of about a
million and a half to 2

00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:18.885
million sensor event
transactions per day.

00:27:18.885 --> 00:27:21.010
What you might not have
noticed also on that slide,

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:24.580
that previous one, that
diagram, was we also do cameras,

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:26.890
especially in the UK.

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:29.530
We do a lot of
numberplate recognition.

00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:33.170
And we're doing about a
million images per week.

00:27:33.170 --> 00:27:35.380
And so that's the
class of computing,

00:27:35.380 --> 00:27:38.320
the capacity of data
that we're processing

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:42.810
through this platform
called SmartCloud.

00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:45.150
The following three things
that I talk about here

00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:49.020
is one of the objectives
of our SmartCloud platform

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:51.090
was to actually
eliminate the need

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:54.510
to have software developers
involved in everything

00:27:54.510 --> 00:27:56.280
that a customer wants.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:59.040
We wanted to enable
the customer to build

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:01.770
their own dashboards,
their live dashboards,

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:04.590
to display them anywhere
that they needed to,

00:28:04.590 --> 00:28:08.280
without requiring us to
go and hack some code.

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:11.790
The same is true for data
analytics and data processing.

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:13.500
And what we have
actually done is

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:16.770
we've used BigQuery
and Data Studio, which

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:18.270
is a fantastic tool.

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:26.839
My analogy is Gmail to email
is Data Studio to big data.

00:28:26.839 --> 00:28:27.630
It's actually that.

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.980
It's free like Gmail is.

00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:31.440
It's an amazing tool.

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:34.860
And I'll show you in a moment
just from some displays

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:38.310
of those sort of reports that
we're generating directly

00:28:38.310 --> 00:28:42.270
from SmartCloud, which is
using BigQuery underneath it.

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:44.220
The same is true
for business rules.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:48.900
For parking, there are
very complex processes

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:51.030
for managing business rules--

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:56.670
time of day when parking can
occur for certain types of uses

00:28:56.670 --> 00:29:00.270
of that parking, when is
enforcement being enabled

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:02.940
and when is it not,
certain car parking spaces

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:06.510
may become non-car
parking spaces at 4:30

00:29:06.510 --> 00:29:08.370
to allow traffic flow.

00:29:08.370 --> 00:29:12.310
All those rules, including
also other things, such as,

00:29:12.310 --> 00:29:13.920
has a payment arrived?

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:16.230
Have they overstayed their
welcome in that parking

00:29:16.230 --> 00:29:19.202
space because they've gone
beyond the maximum period

00:29:19.202 --> 00:29:20.160
you're allowed to park?

00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:22.830
So all those business
rules, which historically we

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:25.540
wrote custom code
into the system

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:29.010
and had some sort of a UI
that you type in some data

00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:31.260
typically into a
database, now what we have

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:34.320
is if-this, then-that
rules engine,

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:38.690
which processes
this continuously.

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:42.300
And I mentioned our mobile apps
for guidance, and payments,

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:43.560
and other things.

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:47.100
In fact, the app area
is just so significant.

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:48.960
We're all users of apps.

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.300
What we also realized
was that you just don't

00:29:51.300 --> 00:29:55.220
want to have yet another app.

00:29:55.220 --> 00:29:58.050
There are a number of
surveys that have already

00:29:58.050 --> 00:30:01.260
shown that after 90 days or
so of getting a smartphone,

00:30:01.260 --> 00:30:03.600
you don't typically
download another app.

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:05.550
And so getting that
footprint onto your phone

00:30:05.550 --> 00:30:07.300
is really, really hard.

00:30:07.300 --> 00:30:09.870
And so what we've built
is an app framework.

00:30:09.870 --> 00:30:14.190
Obviously, we have
full functional apps.

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:18.420
The smart parking app on the
all stores today is our app.

00:30:18.420 --> 00:30:21.180
And a number of cities,
a number of the cities

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:24.540
that I listed there,
have taken that app,

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:28.530
and we've actually branded
it and facilitated it

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:30.960
with functions that is
appropriate for them.

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:33.660
But more interestingly is
taking those components,

00:30:33.660 --> 00:30:36.210
such as the guidance component
or the payment component,

00:30:36.210 --> 00:30:38.190
and embedding it
in your own app.

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:40.500
And so that's the
framework for mobile apps

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:42.840
that we've also developed.

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:44.700
And finally, managed APIs.

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:47.310
APIs is actually
how you integrate,

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:51.150
how you enable
innovation with ourselves

00:30:51.150 --> 00:30:56.550
internally, how you work with
partners and software vendors,

00:30:56.550 --> 00:30:59.040
as well as integration
with city systems.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:04.020
And so, a full suite of managed
APIs, which are-- and in fact,

00:31:04.020 --> 00:31:07.380
is using the Apogee
technology from Google Cloud.

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:07.880
Guess what?

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:14.600
Just want to quickly
now show you,

00:31:14.600 --> 00:31:17.610
if we can switch to the demo--

00:31:17.610 --> 00:31:19.350
yay.

00:31:19.350 --> 00:31:22.670
I must admit we had a little
bit of a network issue

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:24.250
as I sort of walked up.

00:31:24.250 --> 00:31:27.920
But here's a live picture
of one of the cities.

00:31:27.920 --> 00:31:29.990
This is actually
Wellington City,

00:31:29.990 --> 00:31:32.220
the capital of New Zealand.

00:31:32.220 --> 00:31:34.530
So one end of the world.

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:41.980
And what we've got here is,
I'm zooming in, into the city.

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:44.190
These are the
parking bays that are

00:31:44.190 --> 00:31:46.450
around the city of Wellington.

00:31:46.450 --> 00:31:49.960
And I can see that that's
been vacant for 38 minutes.

00:31:49.960 --> 00:31:56.820
This is live data
right from the city.

00:31:56.820 --> 00:32:00.630
And this is just one
aspect of visualization.

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:03.180
And because it's
built into dashboards,

00:32:03.180 --> 00:32:07.110
I can do such things like
push it into full screen mode

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:08.160
and display.

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:12.780
You can put it onto
big screens elsewhere.

00:32:12.780 --> 00:32:16.290
Every one of these
dashboards is just a URL.

00:32:16.290 --> 00:32:19.980
And one of the
powerful things is

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:22.080
I can create my own dashboards.

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:23.470
And there are some default ones.

00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:24.600
There's the data.

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:26.850
SmartZone is what we
call our infrastructure

00:32:26.850 --> 00:32:30.900
area, where we can monitor
and see the infrastructure.

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:33.890
And what you saw early
on in Fraser's slide

00:32:33.890 --> 00:32:39.750
is that sort of map of all
of the cities' activities

00:32:39.750 --> 00:32:40.440
going on.

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:44.340
Well, here what we've got on
of the other what we call tiles

00:32:44.340 --> 00:32:49.080
is actually a 24-hour cycle
of the heat map of the parking

00:32:49.080 --> 00:32:50.190
utilization in the city.

00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:52.140
And this is just
another bit of insight

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:54.130
that we're providing cities.

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:56.640
Now, if you notice,
some of the things that

00:32:56.640 --> 00:32:59.795
prove this is real time, you can
see the numbers changing there.

00:32:59.795 --> 00:33:01.170
Those are parking
events that are

00:33:01.170 --> 00:33:03.294
happening on the other side
of the world right now.

00:33:05.340 --> 00:33:06.190
Here's another city.

00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:09.980
This is Hamilton City
in New Zealand as well.

00:33:09.980 --> 00:33:12.760
And I think I've
lost the other ones.

00:33:12.760 --> 00:33:16.000
I was going to also show a
couple of cities like Cardiff,

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:16.650
and so on.

00:33:16.650 --> 00:33:18.580
So can we go back to our slides?

00:33:21.820 --> 00:33:26.590
So, just going back to
those sort of smart city

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:31.036
requirements, one of the
things that we in Smart Parking

00:33:31.036 --> 00:33:32.410
and in the smart
parking solution

00:33:32.410 --> 00:33:34.868
have to respond to, and pretty
much what Fraser was talking

00:33:34.868 --> 00:33:39.040
about, is this live streaming,
this constant-- this

00:33:39.040 --> 00:33:41.200
instantaneous world
that we live in.

00:33:41.200 --> 00:33:45.340
And this is a change for how
computing needs to be done.

00:33:45.340 --> 00:33:47.860
It's no longer store
something to do a database,

00:33:47.860 --> 00:33:51.190
then retrieve it, do some
sort of processing on it,

00:33:51.190 --> 00:33:53.440
produce a report, and
then store it back

00:33:53.440 --> 00:33:54.850
into a database, et cetera.

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:57.850
Things have to be
instantaneous and responsive.

00:33:57.850 --> 00:34:00.700
For example, if you were
driving down the street

00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:03.310
and you were using Google
Maps, and it took 20 seconds

00:34:03.310 --> 00:34:05.050
to sort of give
you the next turn,

00:34:05.050 --> 00:34:07.450
you'll probably be
on the wrong street

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:10.270
up a tree or
something like that.

00:34:10.270 --> 00:34:14.260
So, this is the new
norm is live streaming.

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:17.380
The efficiency and convenience
I've been talking just

00:34:17.380 --> 00:34:20.469
indicated the things like
apps that we are delivering

00:34:20.469 --> 00:34:23.800
the dashboards, providing those
dashboards to any display,

00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:28.510
whether it be a display on a
street, because it's a URL,

00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:31.110
anywhere there's a browser
that can be displayed.

00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:34.449
And one of the experiments that
we're actually working through

00:34:34.449 --> 00:34:36.790
is just using Android,
Android Things,

00:34:36.790 --> 00:34:40.969
to display that in remote
places onto other displays.

00:34:40.969 --> 00:34:43.230
And this is really
great for providing

00:34:43.230 --> 00:34:47.070
that convenience of information
where it's pertinent.

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:49.270
And a dashboard can
be just, for example,

00:34:49.270 --> 00:34:51.780
how many bays are in
this area of a street,

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:55.239
or on the floor of
a parking building?

00:34:55.239 --> 00:34:58.670
And so this is all about
connecting insights to you.

00:34:58.670 --> 00:35:00.870
And the you I mean
here is, there

00:35:00.870 --> 00:35:02.580
are many different audiences.

00:35:02.580 --> 00:35:05.430
It can be you, the
user of parking.

00:35:05.430 --> 00:35:08.940
But it may also be
the city strategist

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:12.951
who wants to have understanding
of what's actually really going

00:35:12.951 --> 00:35:13.450
on.

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:16.950
Are the uses and
mechanisms that are

00:35:16.950 --> 00:35:21.170
being operated for the parking,
is it optimal for the city?

00:35:21.170 --> 00:35:25.650
And as we all know, it becomes
very, very inconvenient

00:35:25.650 --> 00:35:29.790
and challenging when you're
circling round and round blocks

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:32.250
just trying to find that park.

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:35.269
Now using this real-time
data, accurate data,

00:35:35.269 --> 00:35:36.810
we could take you
right to that park.

00:35:36.810 --> 00:35:39.230
And when it changes, we're
letting you know as well.

00:35:43.310 --> 00:35:46.460
What we're doing is we're
starting to create the ability

00:35:46.460 --> 00:35:48.900
to enable new outcomes.

00:35:48.900 --> 00:35:50.910
And we've done that
both on the technology--

00:35:50.910 --> 00:35:53.810
so rather than using classical
computing, and I actually

00:35:53.810 --> 00:35:58.130
class using standard
traditional servers

00:35:58.130 --> 00:36:01.090
and server OSs as
being classical,

00:36:01.090 --> 00:36:04.160
we have gone
completely serverless,

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:07.640
completely what I call cloud
native for this platform.

00:36:07.640 --> 00:36:10.970
It is essentially the same way
that Gmail works, the same way

00:36:10.970 --> 00:36:12.770
that Google search
works, is how we've

00:36:12.770 --> 00:36:16.550
built SmartCloud, our platform.

00:36:16.550 --> 00:36:21.980
And it's enabling ourselves
and also customers

00:36:21.980 --> 00:36:24.270
to actually achieve
new outcomes.

00:36:24.270 --> 00:36:27.590
And this is driving us
to become more responsive

00:36:27.590 --> 00:36:28.810
with our roadmap.

00:36:28.810 --> 00:36:32.030
Our roadmap is no longer
a 12-month roadmap.

00:36:32.030 --> 00:36:34.910
It's actually dynamic and
changing because the way

00:36:34.910 --> 00:36:36.710
we're implementing
the functionality.

00:36:36.710 --> 00:36:39.020
And the way we're enabling
our customers to also have

00:36:39.020 --> 00:36:41.630
their roadmaps of
functionality is

00:36:41.630 --> 00:36:46.340
in a much more agile, much
more modular and free,

00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:47.950
democratized manner.

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:49.730
Democratized is an
overused word, I think,

00:36:49.730 --> 00:36:52.040
in this conference.

00:36:52.040 --> 00:36:57.890
So here's just a quick view of
the architecture of SmartCloud.

00:36:57.890 --> 00:37:00.890
And as you can see,
it's heavily GCP.

00:37:00.890 --> 00:37:07.220
And as I mentioned,
totally serverless.

00:37:07.220 --> 00:37:12.350
So IoT Core is where we ingest
and we send out messages from

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:15.800
and we also manage these
tens and tens of thousands

00:37:15.800 --> 00:37:19.670
of devices, both the
gateways and the sensors

00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:22.681
that are actually in the streets
or in the parking buildings.

00:37:22.681 --> 00:37:24.680
I don't know if you've
been to parking buildings

00:37:24.680 --> 00:37:28.880
where you see the red green
lights over parking bays.

00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:31.100
It's quite common in
some parts of the world,

00:37:31.100 --> 00:37:37.150
certainly is very
popular in Australasia.

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:39.770
And it changes
the whole dynamic.

00:37:39.770 --> 00:37:41.810
You drive into your
parking building,

00:37:41.810 --> 00:37:43.520
and you literally
will just glance out

00:37:43.520 --> 00:37:48.530
of the periphery of your
vision to see a parking spot.

00:37:48.530 --> 00:37:52.020
And then we put signs
throughout just saying,

00:37:52.020 --> 00:37:53.900
OK, there are five
up on level four,

00:37:53.900 --> 00:37:57.410
and there are 12 up on
level five, and so on.

00:37:57.410 --> 00:37:59.750
And also just being able
to glance out of the side.

00:37:59.750 --> 00:38:01.550
So this sort of
information is all

00:38:01.550 --> 00:38:06.680
being managed by IoT Core,
both the measurement and also

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:11.100
the control of those
indicators, for example.

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:13.970
We're also managing the
data in very dynamic ways.

00:38:13.970 --> 00:38:17.990
I talked about using Big
Query as the fundamental core

00:38:17.990 --> 00:38:19.460
for big data.

00:38:19.460 --> 00:38:23.190
But simultaneously, we're using
Big Table, which is essentially

00:38:23.190 --> 00:38:25.400
the engine underneath
Google's search,

00:38:25.400 --> 00:38:28.850
to also manage those
parking events in real time.

00:38:28.850 --> 00:38:31.590
And then the API, as I
commented about, for dashboards,

00:38:31.590 --> 00:38:35.210
for payments, and
actually any application.

00:38:35.210 --> 00:38:37.310
So, over to you now.

00:38:37.310 --> 00:38:39.860
Hopefully, that's giving
you a taste of what we do.

00:38:39.860 --> 00:38:41.720
The point is that
what we're doing

00:38:41.720 --> 00:38:44.990
is real world IoT
that is trying to

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:49.400
and is actually reinventing the
experience for cities already.

00:38:49.400 --> 00:38:53.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

